ef 17592186045417] [17592186237544 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186235918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237544 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186236195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237544 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186236481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237544 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186236843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237544 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186237332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237544 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237544 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237544 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186236481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237544 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4e7f2d3e-2600-5235-9730-bc2d942eea77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237555 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186236317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237555 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186236476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237555 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186237022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237555 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186237159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237555 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186237392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237555 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237555 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237555 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186237392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237555 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "56faa6f3-ee6e-5488-a8ad-10874ae44edb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237778 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237778 #edb/ref 1069 "q-100089" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237778 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237778 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2t-1,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=3t,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=1-t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237778 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "794f079c-000d-5b83-a34d-0c1430e5e296" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237778 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨2t-1,3t⟩ \\\\  & =(2t-1){|}_{0}\\cdot (3t){|}_{0}+(2t-1){|}_{1}\\cdot (3t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(2(0)-1)\\cdot (3(0))+(2(1)-1)\\cdot (3(1)) \\\\  & =3\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨3t,1-t⟩ \\\\  & =(3t){|}_{0}\\cdot (1-t){|}_{0}+(3t){|}_{1}\\cdot (1-t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(3(0))\\cdot (1-0)+(3(1))\\cdot (1-1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{2}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237780 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237780 #edb/ref 1069 "q-100091" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237780 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237780 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=t-1,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=2,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=t+1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237780 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e68e3d26-aa65-5eb7-b898-7c00e9b7271e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237780 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t-1,t+1⟩ \\\\  & =(t-1){|}_{-1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{-1}+(t-1){|}_{1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-1-1)\\cdot ((-1)+1)+(1-1)\\cdot (1+1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨2,t+1⟩ \\\\  & =(2){|}_{-1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{-1}+(2){|}_{1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(2)\\cdot ((-1)+1)+(2)\\cdot (1+1) \\\\  & =4\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${p}_{2}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269315 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269315 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269315 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0562db51-66a7-5a18-a492-af715264f543" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269536 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269536 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269536 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2018f499-871d-52eb-9dcb-6eb74af91b57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269624 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II, and III" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269624 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269624 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "28183480-cfb9-5dd9-820e-7245cf6418ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270273 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270273 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270273 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "71005342-d55f-5332-b578-95e6f906faab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270613 #edb/ref 1080 "None of the statements are true" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270613 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270613 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "988d29b3-3a9b-5bcf-a853-66f2587681a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270691 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270691 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270691 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a0144298-79ed-5a85-956d-a14aa98d90c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270694 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270694 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270694 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a0c3fac8-64b6-560e-a9cd-252aed9b1291" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270947 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270947 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270947 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "be2dc30f-2e10-5e59-921e-fc1967641251" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271275 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II, and III" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271275 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271275 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e3d033de-73ce-5f72-b75d-36907f8d890e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271394 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271394 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271394 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f223e07f-474f-53ea-9772-c00afbe6a869" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271710 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186269315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271710 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271710 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271710 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186271275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271710 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186271394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271710 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271710 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271710 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186270694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271710 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7165e98a-3520-5bd3-93ee-a5107bf92b82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271753 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186269536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271753 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186269624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271753 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271753 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271753 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271753 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271753 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271753 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186270613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271753 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "89d564bb-a546-5fa5-a0f0-f3c6a4a67e09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272112 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272112 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140500" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272112 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272112 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={e}^{t},\\,{f}_{3}(t)={e}^{-t}\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-1,1]$ of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272112 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0aa2e7ba-284c-52fb-a0ea-9a38be5541e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272112 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{t} & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-1}^{1}-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{e}^{t}{|}_{-1}^{1}-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(e+{e}^{-1})-{e}^{t}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(e+{e}^{-1})-(e-{e}^{-1}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-1}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{-t} & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t=-{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-1}^{1}-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-t{e}^{-t}{|}_{-1}^{1}+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-{e}^{-1}-e)-{e}^{-t}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(-{e}^{-1}-e)-({e}^{-1}-e) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-1}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272132 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272132 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140556" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272132 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272132 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=2(t-1),\\,{f}_{2}(t)={e}^{t},\\,{f}_{3}(t)={e}^{(t-1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[0,2]$ of continuous functions over $[0,2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272132 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1e4b04d4-e7d3-5042-809a-2a3512a7a902" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272132 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨2(t-1),{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2(t-1)({e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2(t-1) & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=2, \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{t} & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2(t-1){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{0}^{2}-{\\int }_{0}^{2}{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(2(t-1){e}^{t}){|}_{0}^{2}-{\\int }_{0}^{2}2{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(2{e}^{2}-(-2))-2{e}^{t}{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2}+2-(2{e}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =4\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨2(t-1),{e}^{(t-1)^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2(t-1){e}^{(t-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={e}^{(t-1)^{2}}{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =e-e \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360981 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360981 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360981 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "21aa3b60-7035-52b5-ae28-6a977fededb5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360997 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360997 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360997 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "257d7e25-c8e7-57e6-a4d4-aa21b511e781" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361170 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361170 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361170 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4cabde88-edb3-52af-8460-89267827bc29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361176 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II, and III" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361176 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361176 #edb/ref 108