76 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362003 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362003 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186361830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362003 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1900c13c-3fc3-5304-bb7b-280cc216d669" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362147 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362147 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362147 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362147 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362147 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362147 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362147 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362147 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186361441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362147 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b5d0cdd2-303c-5ede-bc8f-db210acff19e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362393 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362393 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99839" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362393 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362393 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\,{f}_{3}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[0,\\pi /2]$ of continuous functions over $[0,\\pi /2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\n*Hint: $⟨{f}_{2},{f}_{3}⟩=0.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362393 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4c64aee5-b8e4-5802-9d88-5d5ccb488483" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362393 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+3t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2t)}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)}{4}){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )}{4})-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-5t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+5t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-4t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-4t)}{4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)}{6}){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2\\pi )}{4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )}{6})-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t),$ ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ and ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380400 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186380401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380400 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186380402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380400 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186380403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380400 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186380404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380400 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186380405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380400 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380400 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380400 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186380404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380400 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1f8ff57c-2dd5-5957-95fe-e21b81773d60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380401 #edb/ref 1080 "None of the statements are true" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380401 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380401 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9ecf8e21-9fff-59b4-ba33-fb7d586e4233" #edb/ref 175921860